## Frequently Asked Questions

## Can I File a Lemon Law Claim if I Bought My Car From a Dealership in Glendale But Now Live Somewhere Else?

Yes. California lemon law follows the location of the sale or lease, not where you currently live. If you purchased or leased the vehicle in Glendale and the problem started while it was under warranty, you may still have a valid claim, even if you’ve moved. What matters is where the original purchase happened and whether the defect was reported during the coverage period.

## What Happens if the Dealership Keeps Saying ‘It’s Normal’ But the Issue Isn’t Going Away?

That’s a classic dodge. If the dealership tells you the issue is “normal” but your car continues to stall, shake, leak, or show warning lights, keep pressing and document everything. Manufacturers typically deny problems until pressure is applied. A lemon law attorney can help determine whether the issue meets legal standards and force a real investigation. Don’t rely on the service department’s opinion alone.

## Will Filing a Lemon Law Claim Hurt My Credit or Loan Status?

No. Filing a Glendale lemon law claim is not reported to credit bureaus and doesn’t negatively impact your score. You still need to make loan payments while your case is open, but if you win, your lender is paid off as part of the buyback.

## Can I Still Qualify If My Car is Out of Warranty Now?

Yes, as long as the problem started while the vehicle was still under the manufacturer’s warranty. You don’t have to be under warranty when you file the claim, just when the issue first appeared.

